Carry the boot handoff through `rtkload`: save a0..a3 at `__start`, load
them back in `start_kernel`.

`P6r-10`, 2026-10-23, 144th segment.  The design is `notes/self-update.md`
§ 1.3; this patch is the decompressor's half of the register handoff
`src/rlxboot/jump.S`'s `rlxboot_jump_handoff` starts and
`kernel/linux-2.6.30/arch/rlx/kernel/rlxfw-boot.c` reads.  It is the first
host-compat patch that changes code the CPU executes before the kernel.  The
only other vendor line the handoff needs is the `config/rlxfw-marks.tsv` row
that builds rlxfw's own `rlxfw-boot.c`: the kernel already stores a0..a3 in
`fw_arg0..3` at `kernel_entry`.

WHAT WAS TRUE BEFORE, 讀 in the drop at its pin (`SPEC.md` `FW-261`).
`rlxboot` enters `rtkload` at 0x80500000, and `rtkload` as built loses all
four registers: `start.S` moves the stack top into a0 before `j main`
(`move a0, t0`), the C code that follows uses a0..a3 freely, and
`start_kernel` is `move t0,a0; li a0,0; li a1,0; li a2,0; jr t0; nop`, so the
kernel is entered with a0..a2 zero and a3 whatever `hfload.c` left.  `v1.0`'s
`nfjrom`, disassembled at 0x80500000, holds those six instructions as written,
and a seventh word after them: a second `nop`, the reorder-mode assembler's
own fill of the `jr`'s delay slot.

WHAT THIS CHANGES, three insertions and one replacement, nothing else:

  * `rlxfw_rtk_handoff`: four words in `.data`, aligned to 16 so that they
    are one D-cache line of their own (`cache.c`: 4 words a line), followed
    by the 11-byte witness `RLXFW-RTKH1`.  `.data` and not `.bss`, because
    the loop at `_bstart` clears `.bss` after the save.  `start.o` is first
    in the link, so the array lands at `_fdata`, ahead of the compressed
    kernel, above the decompressor's output (from 0x80000000, ending at or
    below 0x80500000 or `build` refuses the image) and below its heap
    (above `.bss`);
  * at `__start`, before anything else runs: eight instructions that form
    the array's KSEG1 alias and store a0..a3 there;
  * `start_kernel` takes the kernel's entry out of a0 FIRST, loads a0..a3
    back from the same KSEG1 alias and jumps: eleven instructions in place of
    the six source lines.

Both blocks are `.set noreorder` / `.set nomacro` with every delay slot
written: the entry block has no branch and no load; in `start_kernel` each
`lw` is followed by an instruction that does not read its target, and the
`jr`'s slot is a `nop`.  Each block RESTORES `reorder` and `macro` after
itself rather than pushing and popping: `start.S` holds no `.set` directive,
so the vendor's own lines run in gas's default reorder/macro mode and rely on
it (`beq t0, VERSION_D, ...` is a macro), and restoring the defaults leaves
them as they were.  KSEG1 for both the store and the load, so no cache state
decides what `start_kernel` reads: nothing in `rtkload` reads or writes the
array through KSEG0, so its own write-back and invalidate before the jump
have no line of it to act on.  The one assumption kept is `rlxboot`'s: that
its write-back before its own jump leaves no dirty line of the payload copy,
the array's initial zeros included, to land on the array later.

The hunks are generated, not typed: `diff -u` of a copy of the pinned
`start.S` (sha256 `58cb9a79...de46`) against the same copy with the three
blocks inserted by a script that refuses any other file and any anchor that
does not occur exactly once.  The context lines are the drop's own bytes,
trailing blanks included, so applying the patch is the check that they are.

量 2026-10-23, at the desk: regenerated in a fresh directory the hunks are
byte-identical to `notes/self-update.md` § 1.3's; applied the way
`tools/rlxfw-kbuild.sh` applies every patch here, which is
`patch -p1 --forward --silent` from the staged `linux-2.6.30/`, to a copy of
the drop's `rtkload/`, `start.S` becomes sha256 `3661a64a...74b4` with no
offset and no fuzz, and a second apply is refused.  Built by the vendor's
toolchain around `v1.0`'s `vmlinux`, the stub grows by 12 instructions (48
bytes: 8 at the entry, and 11 in place of the 7 words above), the array lands
at `_fdata`, and the `nfjrom` keeps `v1.0`'s 1,110,016 bytes.

WHAT DECIDES WHETHER IT REACHED THE IMAGE.  Not the tree: until this patch
`tools/rtkimage.py build` copied `rtkload/` from the pristine drop, so a
patch here showed as applied in the cell and was absent from the `nfjrom`
(`SPEC.md` `FW-262`).  `build` now copies the cell's `rtkload/` and refuses an
image unless the linked `memload-full` has `rlxfw_rtk_handoff` (16 bytes, on
a 16-byte boundary, in [`_fdata`, `__vmlinux_start`)), the entry `__start`
is 0x80500000, the vendor's five-word zeroing sequence is absent from the
first 16 KiB of `nfjrom` and `RLXFW-RTKH1` occurs in it exactly once -- each
read the same way on the drop's own `memload-full` and `image/nfjrom`, which
must read absent, 1 and 0, or it refuses to judge.  `rlxfw-marks verify`
cannot see any of this: it reads the kernel's ELF and `System.map`, and
`rtkload` is in neither.

PREDICTION, written before any device boot: a slot boot of an image built
from a cell staged with this patch and with the kernel's `rlxfw-boot.c`
linked, by an `rlxboot` that prints `RLXBOOT-HANDOFF slot=X ver=N`, makes
`/proc/rlxfw-boot` read `slot X ver N`.
REFUTED BY `none` there after such a line, or by `rtkimage.py build`'s gate
refusing an image built from such a cell.  An `rlxboot` that zeroes a0..a3
(the rescue copy, the `BOOT=ram` build) makes it read `none` either way: the
fail-closed case, not a refutation.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES NOT ESTABLISH.  That it runs on the die: no instruction
of it has executed anywhere.  That the vendor assembler schedules it as the
host's does: only the artefact the vendor toolchain makes counts, which is
why the gate reads that.  And the cache argument above is an argument --
neither the D side's write policy nor the write-back half of `CCTL 0x200` is
measured on this die (`notes/self-update.md` § 1.5).

--- a/rtkload/start.S
+++ b/rtkload/start.S
@@ -18,9 +18,39 @@
         
 	.extern __start_dram
  
+/* rlxfw (config/host-compat/0011): the boot handoff, P6r-10.
+ * rlxboot (src/rlxboot/jump.S) enters here with a0..a3 = magic, slot,
+ * version, check.  They are saved at entry -- before `move a0, t0`
+ * below puts the stack top in a0 and before main() uses a0..a3 at all --
+ * and loaded back into a0..a3 by start_kernel, which used to zero
+ * a0..a2.  One 16-byte D-cache line of its own (cache.c: 4 words), in
+ * .data and not .bss, because the loop at _bstart below clears .bss.
+ * Written and read only through the KSEG1 alias, so no cache state
+ * decides what start_kernel reads. */
+	.data
+	.align	4
+	.globl	rlxfw_rtk_handoff
+	.type	rlxfw_rtk_handoff, @object
+	.size	rlxfw_rtk_handoff, 16
+rlxfw_rtk_handoff:
+	.word	0, 0, 0, 0
+	.ascii	"RLXFW-RTKH1"
+
         .text
         .globl __start              
 __start:
+	.set	noreorder		/* rlxfw: no branch, no load: no slot */
+	.set	nomacro
+	lui	$8, %hi(rlxfw_rtk_handoff)
+	addiu	$8, $8, %lo(rlxfw_rtk_handoff)
+	lui	$9, 0x2000
+	or	$8, $8, $9		/* KSEG0 -> KSEG1: uncached */
+	sw	$4, 0($8)		/* a0: magic */
+	sw	$5, 4($8)		/* a1: slot */
+	sw	$6, 8($8)		/* a2: version */
+	sw	$7, 12($8)		/* a3: check */
+	.set	macro
+	.set	reorder
 		 
 #ifdef CONFIG_DONOT_ADJUST_SYSCLK_MEMSP
 		nop
@@ -155,12 +185,21 @@
 
         .globl start_kernel
 start_kernel:
-        move t0, a0
-        li a0, 0
-        li a1, 0
-        li a2, 0
-		jr t0
-       	nop 
+	.set	noreorder		/* rlxfw: every slot below is filled by hand */
+	.set	nomacro
+	addu	$8, $4, $0		/* t0 = the kernel entry, out of a0 FIRST */
+	lui	$9, %hi(rlxfw_rtk_handoff)
+	addiu	$9, $9, %lo(rlxfw_rtk_handoff)
+	lui	$10, 0x2000
+	or	$9, $9, $10		/* KSEG0 -> KSEG1: uncached */
+	lw	$4, 0($9)		/* next: lw, reads $9 not $4 */
+	lw	$5, 4($9)		/* next: lw, reads $9 not $5 */
+	lw	$6, 8($9)		/* next: lw, reads $9 not $6 */
+	lw	$7, 12($9)		/* next: jr, reads $8 not $7 */
+	jr	$8
+	nop				/* delay slot: nothing */
+	.set	macro
+	.set	reorder
 
 
